/**
@page tutorial_orm ORM Entity Mapping Tutorial

@tableofcontents

The @c database_system ORM lets you describe a table as a C++ class using
two macros, @c ENTITY_FIELD and @c ENTITY_METADATA. The result is a
type-safe entity that can generate its own @c CREATE @c TABLE statement,
expose typed field accessors, and integrate with the query builders.

This tutorial walks through defining an entity, attaching constraints,
modelling relationships, and feeding entity metadata into a query.

@section orm_concepts Core Concepts

| Concept | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| @c entity_base | Abstract base class with virtual @c table_name(), @c save(), @c load(), @c update(), @c remove() |
| @c ENTITY_FIELD(type, name, constraints) | Declares a typed field with metadata |
| @c ENTITY_METADATA() | Hooks @c initialize_metadata() into the entity |
| @c entity_metadata | Runtime container for fields, used to generate SQL |
| @c field_constraint | Bitset enum: @c primary_key, @c auto_increment, @c not_null, @c unique, @c index, @c default_value |

@note The library currently exposes the @c ENTITY_FIELD macro plus a manual
@c table_name() override. The optional @c ENTITY_TABLE() form simply wraps
the same idiom.

@section orm_define Step 1: Define an Entity

The example below defines a @c user_entity with a primary key, a unique
username and email, and a not-null age. The @c ENTITY_FIELD macro creates
a typed accessor (e.g. @c user.username.get(), @c user.username = "alice")
and registers a @c field_metadata entry.

@code{.cpp}
#include "database/orm/entity.h"

using namespace database;
using namespace database::orm;

class user_entity : public entity_base
{
public:
    using primary_key_type = int64_t;

    std::string table_name() const override { return "users"; }

    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     id,
                 field_constraint::primary_key | field_constraint::auto_increment)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, username,
                 field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::unique)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, email,
                 field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::unique)
    ENTITY_FIELD(int32_t,     age,       field_constraint::not_null)
    ENTITY_FIELD(bool,        is_active, field_constraint::not_null)

    ENTITY_METADATA()

    bool save()   override { return false; }   // implement against your manager
    bool load()   override { return false; }
    bool update() override { return false; }
    bool remove() override { return false; }

private:
    static inline entity_metadata metadata_{"users"};
};

void user_entity::initialize_metadata()
{
    metadata_.add_field(id_metadata_);
    metadata_.add_field(username_metadata_);
    metadata_.add_field(email_metadata_);
    metadata_.add_field(age_metadata_);
    metadata_.add_field(is_active_metadata_);
}
@endcode

Once defined, you can ask the entity to generate its own DDL:

@code{.cpp}
user_entity u;
std::string ddl = u.get_metadata().create_table_sql();
manager->create_query_result(ddl);
@endcode

@section orm_constraints Step 2: Constraints

Constraints are bitwise OR'd onto each field. The most common combinations
are summarized below.

| Constraint | Effect |
|------------|--------|
| @c primary_key | Marks the column as @c PRIMARY @c KEY |
| @c auto_increment | Backend-specific auto-increment (@c SERIAL, @c AUTOINCREMENT, @c AUTO_INCREMENT) |
| @c not_null | Adds @c NOT @c NULL |
| @c unique | Adds @c UNIQUE |
| @c index | Generates a secondary index in @c create_indexes_sql() |
| @c default_value | Field uses its default expression |

@code{.cpp}
class product_entity : public entity_base
{
public:
    std::string table_name() const override { return "products"; }

    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     id,
                 field_constraint::primary_key | field_constraint::auto_increment)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, sku,         field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::unique)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, description, field_constraint::none)
    ENTITY_FIELD(double,      price,       field_constraint::not_null)
    ENTITY_FIELD(int32_t,     category_id, field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::index)

    ENTITY_METADATA()
    /* save/load/update/remove omitted */
private:
    static inline entity_metadata metadata_{"products"};
};
@endcode

@section orm_relationships Step 3: Relationships

The ORM models relationships through foreign-key columns plus indexes;
join logic lives in the query builder. The example below pairs a
@c post_entity with the @c user_entity from above using @c author_id.

@code{.cpp}
class post_entity : public entity_base
{
public:
    std::string table_name() const override { return "posts"; }

    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     id,
                 field_constraint::primary_key | field_constraint::auto_increment)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, title,     field_constraint::not_null)
    ENTITY_FIELD(std::string, body,      field_constraint::none)
    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     author_id, field_constraint::not_null | field_constraint::index)
    ENTITY_FIELD(int64_t,     created_at, field_constraint::not_null)

    ENTITY_METADATA()
    /* save/load/update/remove omitted */
private:
    static inline entity_metadata metadata_{"posts"};
};
@endcode

You can then materialize both schemas in dependency order:

@code{.cpp}
manager->create_query_result(user_entity{}.get_metadata().create_table_sql());
manager->create_query_result(post_entity{}.get_metadata().create_table_sql());
manager->create_query_result(post_entity{}.get_metadata().create_indexes_sql());
@endcode

@note The @c index constraint on @c author_id triggers
@c create_indexes_sql() to emit @c CREATE @c INDEX statements that
accelerate join lookups.

@section orm_querybuilder Step 4: Query Builder Integration

Entity metadata pairs naturally with the @c query_builder to construct
type-safe queries without hard-coded column lists.

@code{.cpp}
#include "database/query_builder.h"

query_builder qb(database_types::postgres);

// Use the metadata-driven table name to avoid stringly-typed bugs
auto sql = qb.select({"id", "username", "email"})
             .from(user_entity{}.table_name())
             .where("is_active", "=", true)
             .order_by("username", sort_order::asc)
             .limit(50)
             .build();

auto rows = manager->select_query_result(sql);
@endcode

To join @c posts and @c users:

@code{.cpp}
auto sql = qb.select({"posts.id", "posts.title", "users.username"})
             .from("posts")
             .join("users", "posts.author_id", "users.id")
             .where("posts.created_at", ">=", int64_t{1700000000})
             .order_by("posts.created_at", sort_order::desc)
             .limit(20)
             .build();
@endcode

@section orm_inspect Inspecting Metadata at Runtime

Iterating over @c entity_metadata::fields() is useful for diagnostics,
schema migration tools, and serialization helpers.

@code{.cpp}
const auto& meta = user_entity{}.get_metadata();
for (const auto& field : meta.fields())
{
    std::cout << field.name() << " : " << field.type_name();
    if (field.is_primary_key()) std::cout << " [PK]";
    if (field.is_unique())      std::cout << " [UNIQUE]";
    if (field.is_not_null())    std::cout << " [NOT NULL]";
    if (field.has_index())      std::cout << " [INDEX]";
    std::cout << "\n";
}
@endcode

@section orm_next Next Steps

- @ref tutorial_quickstart — CRUD basics if you skipped them
- @ref tutorial_backends — How constraints translate per backend
- @ref orm_entity_demo.cpp — Full runnable ORM example
- @ref query_builder_usage.cpp — Query builder reference patterns

*/
